## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a security incident into a comprehensive, end-to-end remediation plan that spans logging hygiene, secrets management, memory safety, access controls, and incident response.

## Key Concepts

- Secrets lifecycle management with short lease expirations
- Logging redaction and tamper-evident logging
- In-memory secret handling and secure memory scrubbing
- Per-tenant isolation and least-privilege access controls
- Incident response playbooks, rollback procedures, and post-mortem hardening

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: redact secrets in logs
function redactSecrets(obj) {
  const SECRET_PATTERNS = [/^(api[_-]?key|secret|token|password)$/i, /vault/i];

  return JSON.stringify(obj, (key, value) => {
    if (SECRET_PATTERNS.some(p => p.test(key))) {
      return '[REDACTED]';
    }
    return value;
  });
}

// Example: secure memory scrubbing
function clearSecretBuffer(buffer) {
  if (buffer && buffer.fill) {
    buffer.fill(0);
  }
}

// Example: tamper-evident log entry
function createLogEntry(secretKey, event, previousHash) {
  const timestamp = Date.now();
  const payload = `${timestamp}|${event}|${secretKey}|${previousHash}`;
  const hash = crypto.createHmac('sha256', process.env.LOG_SECRET)
    .update(payload)
    .digest('hex');

  return { timestamp, event, hash, previousHash };
}
```

## Concept Overview
JWT authentication with RS256 provides asymmetric cryptography for enhanced security. Refresh token rotation prevents replay attacks by generating new refresh tokens on each use.

## Implementation Details
- **RS256 Algorithm**: Uses private/public key pair instead of shared secret
- **Access Token**: 15-minute expiration, contains user ID and role
- **Refresh Token**: 7-day expiration, stored in httpOnly secure cookie
- **Token Rotation**: New refresh token issued each time, old one invalidated

## Code Example
```javascript
// Generate JWT with RS256
const token = jwt.sign(
  { userId: user.id, role: user.role },
  privateKey,
  { algorithm: 'RS256', expiresIn: '15m' }
);

// Refresh token rotation
app.post('/refresh', async (req, res) => {
  const oldRefreshToken = req.cookies.refresh_token;
  const newTokens = await generateNewTokens(oldRefreshToken);
  await invalidateRefreshToken(oldRefreshToken);
  res.cookie('refresh_token', newTokens.refreshToken, {
    httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: 'strict'
  });
});
```

## Common Pitfalls
- Using HS256 instead of RS256 (shared secret vulnerability)
- Storing refresh tokens in localStorage (XSS risk)
- Not implementing proper token rotation
- Missing secure cookie flags
- Forgetting to invalidate old refresh tokens

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly security thinking around user-generated content in a chat app; it covers validation, malware scanning, storage isolation, and user feedback.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation: MIME vs extension, size caps
- Safe storage: per-user paths, UUIDs, signed URLs
- Malware scanning: pre-publish check and quarantine
- Access control and auditing: per-object ACLs, immutable logs

## Code Example
```javascript
const MAX_SIZE = 5 * 1024 * 1024; // 5 MB
const ALLOWED = new Set(['image/png','image/jpeg','application/pdf']);
app.post('/upload', (req,res)=>{
  const f = req.file;
  if (!f || f.size > MAX_SIZE) return res.status(400).send('Invalid size');
  if (!ALLOWED.has(f.mimetype)) return res.status(400).send('Unsupported type');
  const key = `uploads/${user.id}/${uuid()}${ext(f.originalname)}`;
  // upload to storage, then scan
  storage.put(key, f.buffer).then(()=> scanService.scan(key)).then(r=>{
    if (r.clean) return res.json({url: signer.sign(key)});
    // on fail
    storage.delete(key); res.status(400).send('malware detected');
  }).catch(()=> res.status(500).send('error'));
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle scalable malware scanning for peak traffic?
- What changes would you make for privacy when scanning contents?
- How would you implement a rollback path if a user re-uploads a clean file after a scan failure?

## Why This Is Asked
To assess practical knowledge of session security with minimal risk and knowledge of CSRF.

## Key Concepts
- HttpOnly and SameSite cookies
- CSRF protection strategies (double-submit, token rotation)
- Token rotation for refresh tokens
- Minimal server middleware for validation
- Secure login/logout flows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Express middleware for cookie-based auth
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
function authMiddleware(req, res, next){
  const token = req.cookies?.access_token;
  if(!token) return res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  try {
    req.user = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    // rotation logic if near expiry would be here
    next();
  } catch {
    res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this against XSS and CSRF tooling?
- What trade-offs exist between SameSite settings and subdomain SPAs?

